  "title": {
    "en": "Redshanks and sandpipers"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Redshank, Spotted Redshank, Greenshank, Green, Wood and Common Sandpiper, and Turnstone"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "The medium waders are the ones most often confused in flight. The key is the rump and the wing: a white rump patch, a white trailing edge, a white wedge up the back. All seven birds in this lesson show a different pattern when they fly up, and a different call.\n\nSeason: adults of most of these leave their northern breeding grounds as early as late June, so July and August are good months, and the juveniles follow in September. Common Sandpipers breed along rivers in the north and west and return in April and again in the autumn. Spotted Redshanks pass in spring in their black summer plumage, and in autumn they are pale grey.\n\nLook and listen: the calls of these waders are some of the easiest to learn, and they work better than the plumages. Try to say the call out loud when you hear it."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "trailing edge"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The back edge of the wing. A broad white one shows in flight in the Redshank."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "rump"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The lower back, just above the tail. White in Green and Wood Sandpipers, and in Greenshank a white wedge runs up the back."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "supercilium"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The pale stripe above the eye. Long in Wood Sandpiper and Spotted Redshank."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "flight call"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The call a bird gives when it takes off or flies over. For many waders this is the best way to identify them."
      }
    }
  ],
